## Why This Is Asked
Streaming data often requires maintaining a running statistic under insertions and deletions. The median is robust and a common interview target for dynamic data structures.

## Key Concepts
- Two-heap approach: keep lower half in a max-heap and upper half in a min-heap.
- Duplicates supported; lazy deletion via a delay map.
- Rebalancing invariant: size difference at most 1; median derived from heap tops.

## Code Example
```javascript
class MedianDS {
  constructor(){
    this.lo = new MaxHeap(); // lower half
    this.hi = new MinHeap(); // upper half
    this.delayed = new Map();
    this.sizeLo = 0;
    this.sizeHi = 0;
  }
  _cleanTop(h) {
    const top = h.peek();
    while (top !== null && this.delayed.get(top) > 0) {
      h.pop();
      this.delayed.set(top, this.delayed.get(top) - 1);
    }
  }
  add(x){
    if (this.sizeLo === 0 || x <= this.lo.peek()) {
      this.lo.push(x); this.sizeLo++;
    } else { this.hi.push(x); this.sizeHi++; }
    this._balance();
  }
  remove(x){
    const loTop = this.lo.peek();
    if (loTop !== null && x <= loTop) {
      this.delayed.set(x, (this.delayed.get(x) || 0) + 1);
      this.sizeLo--;
      if (x === loTop) this._cleanTop(this.lo);
    } else {
      this.delayed.set(x, (this.delayed.get(x) || 0) + 1);
      this.sizeHi--;
      this._cleanTop(this.hi);
    }
    this._balance();
  }
  _balance(){
    if (this.sizeLo > this.sizeHi + 1) {
      this.hi.push(this.lo.pop()); this.sizeLo--; this.sizeHi++;
    } else if (this.sizeHi > this.sizeLo) {
      this.lo.push(this.hi.pop()); this.sizeHi--; this.sizeLo++;
    }
    this._cleanTop(this.lo); this._cleanTop(this.hi);
  }
  getMedian(){
    if (this.sizeLo === 0) return null;
    if (this.sizeLo > this.sizeHi) return this.lo.peek();
    return (this.lo.peek() + this.hi.peek()) / 2;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem evaluates the ability to design a practical dynamic sequence with non-trivial frequency queries—a common requirement in analytics and data processing systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Sqrt decomposition** for dynamic sequences partitions data into blocks of approximately √n elements
- **Per-block frequency maps** enable O(√n) rangeFreq queries
- **Block maintenance**: splits when blocks overflow and merges when they underflow to maintain balance
- **Edge handling**: properly handles partial blocks during queries and updates

## Code Example
```javascript
class Block {
  constructor() {
    this.values = [];
    this.freq = new Map();
  }

  rebuild() {
    this.freq.clear();
    for (const v of this.values) {
      this.freq.set(v, (this.freq.get(v) || 0) + 1);
    }
  }
}

class DynamicSequence {
  constructor() {
    this.blocks = [];
    this.B = Math.ceil(Math.sqrt(10));
  }

  locate(index) {
    let offset = 0;
    for (const block of this.blocks) {
      if (offset + block.values.length > index) {
        return { block, pos: index - offset };
      }
      offset += block.values.length;
    }
  }

  append(value) {
    if (this.blocks.length === 0) this.blocks.push(new Block());
    const last = this.blocks[this.blocks.length - 1];
    last.values.push(value);
    last.freq.set(value, (last.freq.get(value) || 0) + 1);
    if (last.values.length > 2 * this.B) this.split(last);
  }

  split(block) {
    const mid = Math.floor(block.values.length / 2);
    const newBlock = new Block();
    newBlock.values = block.values.splice(mid);
    block.rebuild();
    newBlock.rebuild();
    this.blocks.push(newBlock);
  }

  rangeFreq(l, r, value) {
    let count = 0;
    const { block: startBlock, pos: startPos } = this.locate(l);
    const { block: endBlock, pos: endPos } = this.locate(r);

    if (startBlock === endBlock) {
      for (let i = startPos; i <= endPos; i++) {
        if (startBlock.values[i] === value) count++;
      }
      return count;
    }

    for (let i = startPos; i < startBlock.values.length; i++) {
      if (startBlock.values[i] === value) count++;
    }
    for (const block of this.blocks) {
      if (block === startBlock || block === endBlock) continue;
      count += block.freq.get(value) || 0;
    }
    for (let i = 0; i <= endPos; i++) {
      if (endBlock.values[i] === value) count++;
    }
    return count;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests expertise in fully dynamic Li Chao trees, offline lifetime decomposition, and practical handling of duplicates in production settings.

## Key Concepts
- **Segment tree over time** for lifetime interval decomposition
- **Per-node Li Chao trees** for minimum query aggregation
- **Path-query traversal** across O(log N) nodes
- Memory-time trade-offs for handling large N and duplicates

## Code Example
```javascript
class LiChao {
  insert(line) { /* insert line into this node's Li Chao tree */ }
  query(x) { /* return minimum y at x across stored lines */ }
}

class DynamicLineSet {
  constructor(N) {
    this.segmentTree = new SegmentTree(N);
  }

  addLine(m, b, id, insertTime) {
    const interval = [insertTime, Infinity];
    this.segmentTree.addInterval(interval, { m, b, id });
  }

  removeLine(id, removeTime) {
    // Update the line's interval end time in the segment tree
    this.segmentTree.closeInterval(id, removeTime);
  }

  queryMin(x, queryTime) {
    return this.segmentTree.queryPath(queryTime)
      .reduce((min, lichao) => Math.min(min, lichao.query(x)), Infinity);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| addLine | O(log N) | O(log N) per line |
| removeLine | O(log N) | - |
| queryMin | O(log² N) | - |

## Edge Cases
- **Identical slopes**: Stored separately with unique ids; Li Chao tree handles min correctly
- **Immediate removal**: Assigned to leaf node only
- **Large N**: Memory scales with O(N log N) total line-node assignments
